[Music]  everybody knows a great movie when they  see it but sometimes it's hard to put  your finger on what exactly made it work  we usually judge effective moments based  on their shot composition character  choices or dialogue but sometimes  powerful thematic choices come in  unexpected ways in the opening scene of  the Godfather the light hits Amerigo in  a way that makes him look like a rat  emphasizing his betrayal of the Italian  Way of life but the most powerful moment  comes later in the scene it took over a  minute to understand that America is at  odds with his Italian identity but for  Vito it takes us only a tiny moment to  learn just as much about him all it took  was a wave of his hand  like many cinematic techniques a hand  can convey a lot of visual information  depending on the context it can  symbolize power the unknown or affection  and you can even see it in action 80  years ago in 1936 Chevrolet released a  sponsored documentary film titled master  hands showing the work that goes behind  making a Chevrolet car the film focuses  on the hands of anonymous workers as  they build a car from beginning to end  from the master hands of the tool makers  machine at the end of the documentary  hands clutched the newly finished car  and drive off the film shoots the hands  on the steering wheel the same way it  shot the hands making the car and this  association of hands sends a clear  message to consumers  Chevy's are sold to the same blue-collar  hands that also make them carrying their  producers of the car with the consumers  of the car also supports the idea that a  worker directly benefits from the work  they put in but in 1936 the workers  didn't feel that way the mid-1930s  actually had some of the most violent  worker strikes in American history in  his article hands off the machine  historian max Fraser talks about the use  of hands in the art of the 1930s to  symbolize the working class the  instincts to associate hands with the  working class makes sense  most low-paying jobs required tedious  manual labor and artists saw the effect  of those jobs in people's hands in media  at the time there are generally two  types of hands in master hands they show  the benefits of manual labor  they look productive and efficient  helping to create a car in the end in  film hands don't challenge their  mistreatment or act on their own the  hands are almost extensions of the  machines themselves repetitive and  complacent in their singular tasks but  those hands were much more oppressed  than the documentary implies the workers  filmed in master hands actually went on  strike the following year now the hands  are the second type not working hands  but protesting hands free to move as  they please a hands not performing  manual labor has a completely different  symbolic meaning than the working one  instead of doing tasks for a corporation  these hands act on their own free to do  what they want it makes sense that these  free hands became a symbol that labor  parties and communists embraced when a  free Hinch doesn't perform work its  resisting the status quo these two kinds  of hands reflect the level of control  that a person has either their hands do  what they're told like in master hands  or they resist and act on their own like  during a workers strike and we see this  duel symbolism of the hand in feature  film because a hand is such a good way  to show a person acting on their own  self-interest a hand oftentimes  represents a person's control or a lack  of control of course hands and movies  don't always have to contain some grand  symbolic meaning but if you approach  them from this angle you may see some  patterns in how they're used every human  has hands so it's a pretty accessible  symbol in popular culture that  filmmakers use in a variety of contexts  the symbolic power of hands is almost  unlimited  [Music]  visually speaking the force essentially  functions as hand superpowers Vader  chokes an adversary from afar with just  a small pinch of his fingers and uses  his hands to stop lasers in their tracks  the force extends the users physical  control into their environments not only  are you in control of yourself but you  can literally control others it's the  ultimate way to show power  so if hands give people control then  losing a hand does the opposite losing  your hand in a Star Wars film means much  more because hands are such a symbol of  power without his hand we feel unsure if  Luke will regain the same mastery over  the force that he had earlier it's the  total loss of control of his hands that  contributes to the feeling of defeat at  the end of an empire while Vader wants  to sever Luke's powerful hand  Sauron wants to empower the hand in Lord  of the Rings the most powerful tool is  not a crown or a necklace it's a ring a  ring empowers a person's hand in the  same way that a tool raised in the air  empowers a factory worker but ultimately  a hand wearing a ring is not a free hand  and by creating desire for power the  ring ironically enslaves not unlike the  factory workers under the control of  their company fantasy films use hands  and the extension of a person's hands to  show power or the lack of it and that  includes the most popular franchise in  recent memory like the ring and the  force the Infinity Gauntlet gets a lot  of its symbolic strength by focusing on  the hand the gauntlet feels like one of  the most intimidating weapons put to  film because it extends the control of a  hand into control of the fabric of the  entire universe it's certainly cool that  Thanos can use the gauntlet to control  moons you throw another moon at me I'm  gonna lose it but we see his power the  most when he does so much more with such  a little gesture with a simple snap of  his fingers he alters the course of the  entire universe  it's basically the opening of the  Godfather on steroids both fan OHS and  Vito essentially used their hands in the  same way before Vito speaks he gives a  command with his hands to get Amerigo a  drink with such a small gesture he can  control the world around him the hand  movement shows Vito's power precisely  because the gesture is so small by  placing the hand in the foreground it  dominates the scene and conveys visual  Authority when Vito does his gesture  Amerigo is literally in the palm of his  hands paul thomas anderson uses a  similar method in there will be blood he  often centers the frame on the hand to  subtly communicate important bits of  information while a different film might  cut to close-ups of faces or the map  anderson has the confidence to stick to  this wide shot and use hands to direct  our eyes and if anyone's hands command  attention throughout the scene it's dan  use he doesn't even have to directly  face the camera to command the screen  when he goes to shake Paul's hand his  hand cuts across the frame preventing  Eli from leaving this motion feels  aggressive despite just being a  handshake it's a small example of  Daniels manipulative personality  disguised as just the simple work of a  businessman throughout the rest of the  film Daniels hands are a main focus for  his character he regularly points grabs  and slaps  and he always does so to express his  authority the film follows the power  struggle between him and Eli so it makes  sense that they use their hands to  attempt to take control and this scene  Daniel asserts his authority and Eli  stands by looking powerless with his  hands a little lower than the center of  the frame in many ways they are like the  hands of America also tightly contained  the one big difference though is that  americo's hands tremble and move around  randomly while Eli remains steady it  turns out that Eli was only holding back  and we see the true power of his hands  when he leads Church here he has the  authority to point grab and even slap  Daniel at this point in the film Eli has  just as much power but of course it's  Daniel who wins in the end  and once again his hands dominate the  frame I drink your milkshake but  thinking about hands only as a symbol of  a power dynamic and sells them short  many of the most famous hands in movie  histories don't necessarily fit into  that idea the problem is that we've been  thinking of hands as an extension of the  power of a human but what if the hand  isn't even human in the first place and  what if the hand isn't even attached to  anything there's a common horror trope  of the severed hand that still moves  there's something frightening about a  hand without its master  possibly because it literally has a mind  of its own if a hand represents control  then loosing control of your hands would  be the ultimate loss of agency unusual  hands make their way into the horror  genre because we all have an  understanding of what a hand should be  and it's the part of our body we use the  most for everyday tasks an understanding  of what's normal and common gives the  filmmaker the opportunity to show that  someone or something is abnormal in  district 9 wicked starts turning into an  alien after accidentally spraying  himself with a mysterious liquid so  which body part becomes alien first  and as expected his hands changing means  he can now fire alien weapons not only  do his hands represent the abnormal but  they still show the classic power  dynamic ET introduces the idea that an  alien hand doesn't have to be evil  before ET aliens almost always appeared  as the villain and their foreign hands  meant they were a threat to the norm but  each uses his hands to show his trust  and Eliot  like King Kong it challenges the  assumption that a foreign hand has to be  an evil one we've been ignoring the most  important reason for hands the kind of  hands we hopefully see most often in our  everyday lives the trusting hands hands  are an easy way to visualize cooperation  it goes against the idea that hands are  used to dominate a situation they can  also show cooperation and affection hand  touching isn't always cold and  power-hungry like the kiss of death a  movie like Call Me by your name feels so  warm and loving because characters  regularly touch and hold each other and  not just in a romantic way Ellie OS dad  speaks to him in a surprisingly tender  way about his relationship and as the  scene fades out he reaches out to touch  him in contrast the dad and American  Beauty never touches anybody once he  finally does it's in such an awkward and  unfulfilled way or maybe the dad's hand  is too controlling  oh my fin old my fin Marlin in Finding  Nemo has to accept that he can never  entirely control and protect Nemo we see  his conflict manifested literally when  he holds on to the whales tongue but  instead of a hand  it's a flipper  so hands really are the visible part of  the brain if some people literally use  their hands as a language I don't think  it's much of a stretch to think that  filmmakers can use hands to communicate  complicated ideas control good or the  lack of it  the foreign or the familiar the loving  or the sassy ones it's definitely a  weird way to look at a scene but who  knows maybe one day it'll come in handy  I'm not sure what to do with my hand be  good just hold him down by your side  yeah great of course the symbolic  history of hands goes back much further  in other art forms there's a fascinating  history behind the study of hands in art  during the Renaissance era artists like  Michelangelo and da Vinci had a fixation  on the anatomy of the hand that was both  scientific and aesthetic I definitely  recommend checking out the documentary  the Renaissance factor because it goes  into details beyond the scope of this  video it's a fascinating watch that  outlines the advances in organ science  that prompted this focus on Anatomy  you can watch it on curiosity straight  curiosity streams newly expanded library  has thousands of titles that will send  you down the rabbit hole on topics like  arts science and history go to curiosity  stream comm slash now you see it and  enter the promo code now you see it when  prompted during the signup process your  membership is completely free for the  first 30 days with our code plus it  helps us out too and as always thanks to  our patrons for your support if you're  considering supporting the channel check  out our patreon to get early access to  videos and behind-the-scenes content  thanks for watching  [Music]

